@startuml qnx_dispatch_engine_commands_activity
title QnxDispatchEngine - command scheduling and timer dispatch

start
if (Entry is EnqueueCommand?) then (yes)
    :RegisterTimedEntry()\nassign entry deadline, owner and moved callback;
    :Lock the queue mutex;
    :Insert before the first strictly later deadline\nor at the end if none exists;
    :Unlock the queue mutex;
    :WakeUpTimerQueue()\nsend kTimerPulseCode to the client side channel\nMsgSendPulse result is unchecked;
    :Return to the submitting caller;
    stop
else (timer pulse)
    :TimerPulseCallback invokes ProcessTimerQueue()\non the client dispatch thread;
endif

:Snapshot now = Clock::now();
:ProcessQueue(now)\nlock the queue mutex;
while (Queue is non-empty?) is (yes)
    :Read the front entry deadline;
    if (Deadline is later than now?) then (yes)
        :Unlock and return that deadline as then;
        :Compute nanoseconds(then - Clock::now()) + 1;
        :Set a one-shot timer using that value cast to uint64_t\nTimerSettime result is unchecked;
        stop
    else (due)
        :Unlink the entry and move its callback to a local;
    endif
    :Unlock the queue mutex;
    :Invoke the callback with the original now snapshot;
    :Destroy the local callback and its captures;
    :Relock the queue mutex;
endwhile (no)
:Unlock and return TimePoint{};
:Return without rearming or disarming the timer;
stop

legend
    TimedCommandQueue::ProcessQueue is expanded in the pulse path.
    Equal deadlines retain insertion order; TimePoint{} denotes immediate work.
    A callback can requeue its unlinked entry while the mutex is released.
    The view records the timer arithmetic, not a latency or deadline guarantee.
endlegend

@enduml
